window.__BUNDLE__={"generatedAt":"2026-10-26T20:47:18.367318+00:00","recentDays":7,"archiveDays":1825,"recentCutoff":"2026-10-19","archiveCutoff":"2021-10-27","addedToday":0,"addedThisRun":0,"fetchStats":{"hf":335,"arxiv":1434,"total":1769},"freshness":{"latestPaperDate":"2026-10-26","latestPublishedDate":"2026-10-26","latestBatchCount":257,"newInLast24h":290,"newInLast48h":290},"historyTotal":27309,"topicCounts":{"Multi-Agent":2983,"Evaluation":14922,"Planning":2013,"Memory":533,"Code Agent":1088,"Interpretability":922,"Agentic RAG":1487,"Agentic Search":1977,"RL for LLM":1474,"Reasoning":7393,"Hallucination":1227,"Post-Training":3087,"Alignment":3596,"World Model":539,"Tool Use":1420,"Agent Safety":2668,"Computer Use":366,"Reflection":984,"VLM Training":2129,"LLM Training":3491,"Fine-tuning":4222,"Multimodal Agent":157,"Agent Framework":931,"Error Attribution":178,"Data Engine":547},"count":36,"recentTotal":2409,"archiveCount":0,"archiveTotal":24900,"sources":{"hf":66,"arxiv":2343,"semanticScholar":0},"archiveSources":{"hf":0,"arxiv":0,"semanticScholar":0},"warnings":[],"notes":["archive is split by year under data/archive/ for fast lazy loading; data/history.json keeps the complete database"],"papers":[{"upvotes":34,"title":"RSIHub: A Composable Framework for Agent-Evolution Methods","topics":["Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32253","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"hf","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32253","authors":["Zimu Wang","Xiaobo Wang","Siyu Ye","Jiaqi Li","Silin Chen","Yuling Shi","Ruobing Wang","Jingjing Zhang","Chaofan Wang","Kai Wu","Mengnan Qi","Kai Cai","Xiaodong Gu","Zilong Zheng"],"tags":["Evaluation"],"date":"2026-10-26","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.32253","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32253.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32253","abstract":"Agent-evolution methods use execution feedback to improve prompts, skills, tools, and orchestration, but their implementations often rebuild the same experimental machinery. We present RSIHub, an open-source framework for building, comparing, and extending these methods on shared infrastructure. Recipes compose reusable operators and declare mutable resources; RSIHub supplies execution, evaluation, recovery, and lineage. Researchers can focus on evolution policy while evaluation stays outside the mutable surface and results remain tied to candidates and evaluation conditions. We instantiate AHE, Hyperagents, A-Evolve, and GEPA without modifying the framework core, keeping method-specific logic in recipes and operators. We evaluate them with MiniSWE and Codex on Terminal Bench 2 and $τ^3$ Banking under fixed model and evaluator configurations. The largest observed overall pass@1 gain is 1"},{"upvotes":31,"title":"RynnUnify: Towards Unified Embodied Brain","topics":["Reasoning","Planning","RL for LLM"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32965","source":"hf","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32965","authors":["Minghao Zhu","Bohan Hou","Kun Xiang","Zixuan Wang","Yinzhou Tang","Yifei Yang","Zhikai Wang","Tianyi Zhang","Kehan Li","Haoyu Zhao","Siteng Huang","Leigang Qu","Sicong Leng","Dongchi Huang","Mingxiu Chen","Shijian Lu","Yue Wang","Xiaodan Liang","Zhenyu Zeng","Deli Zhao","Chengju Liu","Jianfei Yang","Xin Li"],"tags":["Reasoning","Planning","RL for LLM"],"date":"2026-10-26","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.32965","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32965.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32965","abstract":"We introduce RynnUnify, a 16B unified embodied model that combines strong embodied understanding with long-horizon visual planning through interleaved video--language reasoning. With capabilities spanning spatial reasoning, physical prediction, and grounding, RynnUnify connects an understanding of the physical world with visual anticipation of how it evolves across successive subtasks. Building on these capabilities, we further develop an agentic extension through reinforcement learning to selectively use video predictions for reasoning about unobserved states and interaction outcomes. We also develop a world-action extension of the full RynnUnify model, translating its understanding and prediction capabilities into robot control. To evaluate these capabilities, we introduce Rynn-VP and Rynn-GEU for long-horizon visual planning and anticipatory reasoning. Experiments show that RynnUnify "},{"upvotes":9,"title":"FLOMSS: Generative Music Source Separation by Flow Matching","topics":["Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17821","source":"hf","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17821","authors":["Shuyang Cui","Zhi Zhong","Keisuke Toyama","Koichi Saito","Qiyu Wu","Yukara Ikemiya","Junghyun Koo","Kin Wai Cheuk","Woosung Choi","Giorgio Fabbro","Wei-Hsiang Liao","Shusuke Takahashi","Yuki Mitsufuji"],"tags":["Evaluation"],"date":"2026-10-26","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.17821","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.17821.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.17821","abstract":"Music source separation (MSS) has been conventionally addressed by discriminative methods. Recently, flow matching has emerged as a strong generative paradigm for modeling complex data distributions, with successful applications across diverse domains. These advances motivate us to systematically investigate flow matching for MSS in a four-source (Vocals, Bass, Drums, Other) setting in full-band stereo. Specifically, we investigate prediction targets, flow paths, single-source versus multi-source modeling, and mixture consistency constraints. Beyond the signal-level metrics commonly used for MSS, such as signal-to-distortion ratio, we further evaluate MSS methods using embedding-based metrics, as well as a listening test, providing a comprehensive benchmark. On MUSDB18-HQ, our proposed model exhibits competitive performance in both objective and subjective tests, highlighting the potenti"},{"upvotes":11,"title":"On-Policy Self-Training: Data-Free Self-Improvement in Large Language Models","topics":["Post-Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","Reflection"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32770","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32770","authors":["Leon Sick","Lukas Hoyer","Yannick Strümpler","Christian Reisswig","Timo Ropinski","Federico Tombari"],"tags":["Post-Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","Reflection"],"date":"2026-10-26","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.32770","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32770.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32770","abstract":"On-policy self-distillation enables post-training foundation models without relying on stronger external teachers. However, existing approaches rely on ground-truth question-answer pairs, human-curated problem sets, or other supervised signals. In this work, we reformulate on-policy self-distillation as a self-training framework, driven by self-generated question-answer pairs and privileged reasoning demonstrations. We push supervision to the limit: guided solely by coarse, high-level topic descriptions, the model synthesizes its own curriculum of queries and privileged reasoning traces without access to any human-curated prompts or answers. We find that self-distillation on fully synthetic privileged information enables a model to improve across multiple domains while retaining its capabilities on domains it is not self-trained on. We demonstrate the generality of this framework across "},{"upvotes":11,"title":"DraMaze: A Multi-Agent Pipeline with Future-Aware Memory for Diverse Microdrama Script Generation","topics":["Multi-Agent","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32002","submittedDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32002","authors":["Siyang He","Qiqi Wang","Fan Liu","Yuncheng Huang","Pengfei Xu","Xi Chen","Ziwei He","Xipeng Qiu"],"tags":["Multi-Agent","Evaluation"],"date":"2026-10-26","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.32002","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32002.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32002","abstract":"The rapid growth of microdramas has increased demand for high-quality script production. Long-form microdrama script generation with LLMs requires both diverse plot progression and long-range narrative coherence. Our observations reveal that candidate continuations increasingly concentrate on fewer narrative mechanisms as generation progresses, while conventional retrieval may reinforce trope reuse and overlook historical information essential to future plots. To address these challenges, we propose DraMaze, a multi-agent pipeline that explores diverse causal paths through trope memory-guided evolutionary tree search and mutation. It further constructs a Backward Narrative Dependency Tree to organize, retain, and retrieve historical memories based on future narrative goals. We also introduce MiDramaBench, a bilingual benchmark for evaluating script logic and creative quality across diffe"},{"upvotes":3,"title":"PaperFormalizer: Author-in-the-Loop Autoformalization of Research Papers in Lean","topics":["Code Agent"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31810","submittedDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31810","authors":["Jiyuan Tan","Vasilis Syrgkanis","Jose Blanchet"],"tags":["Code Agent"],"date":"2026-10-26","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.31810","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.31810.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.31810","abstract":"Even a carefully proofread paper can leave small gaps in its proofs or its stated assumptions. We introduce PaperFormalizer, a Lean-based agentic pipeline that helps authors find and close such gaps in their own papers and turn each paper into a Lean-verified version. Starting from the LaTeX source, PaperFormalizer transcribes the paper into a dependency graph of statements, audits each statement in natural language for faithfulness, ambiguity and correctness, and then formalizes and proves the statements in Lean against Mathlib, optional domain libraries and the author's personal toolkit. Only the author knows what a statement is meant to say and how a formalization gap in a result is best fixed. The pipeline therefore stops and asks the author whenever a statement admits more than one reading, presents every repair, in natural language and TeX symbols, as a proposal that the author can"},{"upvotes":3,"title":"LoCA: Enabling Multi-Modality in Geospatial Location Encoders","topics":["Alignment"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32894","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32894","authors":["Panagiotis Koromilas","Eleanna Vali","Diogenis Antonopoulos","Eleni Saka","Dimitris Christofis","Nikolaos Ioannis Bountos","Konstantin Klemmer","Ioannis Papoutsis","Yannis Panagakis","Mihalis A. Nicolaou"],"tags":["Alignment"],"date":"2026-10-26","hfUrl":"https://huggingface.co/papers/2610.32894","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32894.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32894","abstract":"Location encoders map geographic coordinates to high-dimensional descriptors through contrastive alignment with paired Earth Observation imagery. We prove that this construction is structurally limited: any Lipschitz location encoder produces an output of intrinsic dimension at most 2, regardless of architecture or bandwidth, which forces the contrastive loss to strictly exceed its asymptotic optimum. Empirically, joint training also collapses the encoder's embedding covariance to low rank. Although dimension-limited, current location encoders sidestep this spectral collapse by training against a frozen image encoder, which we reinterpret as a covariance regularizer that supplies a high-rank target for the location encoder to fold onto. However, extending this approach to multimodal inputs is challenging, as location encoders should simultaneously map a single location to multiple, poten"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"Cycle-of-Thought: Self-Evaluative Iterative Reasoning for Table Visual Question Answering in Document Images","topics":["LLM Training","VLM Training","Reasoning","Hallucination","Evaluation","Alignment","Fine-tuning","Reflection"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32423","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32423","authors":["Somraj Gautam","Gaurav Harit"],"tags":["LLM Training","VLM Training","Reasoning","Hallucination","Evaluation"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32423.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32423","abstract":"Recent advances in Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have significantly improved Visual Question Answering (VQA) performance, yet their reasoning over structured content such as tables remains inconsistent, often plagued by spatial misalignment and logical hallucinations. Existing approaches for improving table VQA typically rely on architectural modifications or expensive supervised fine-tuning (SFT), which impose high barriers to entry due to the need for large-scale, expert-annotated datasets and significant computational resources. In this work, we propose Self-RefineVQA, a training-free, inference-time self-correction framework that improves table VQA performance without additional labeled data or model updates. Inspired by human revision behavior, our method enables a single VLM to iteratively act as a generator, critic, and refiner, forming a closed self-feedback loop that corrects co"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"Kolibri: A Sovereign European Model on the Pareto Frontier","topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Tool Use","Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning","RL for LLM"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31945","submittedDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31945","authors":["Aleph Alpha",":","Moritz Althaus","Artur Baranowski","Michael Barlow","Fabien C. Y. Benureau","Felix Berkenkamp","Amrita Bhattacherjee","Lukas Blübaum","Duncan Blythe","Augustijn de Boer","Bastian Boll","Thomas F. Burns","Björn Deiseroth","Niko Dürr","Béni Balázs Egressy","Niklas Finken","Niklas Freymuth","Fabian Gebhart","Max Hammer","Bastian Harren","Andreas Hartel","Johann Higl","Steffen Hirschmann","Pablo Iyu Guerrero","Yasser Jadidi","Andreas Jagusch","Mitja Kleider","Markus Klein","Jonas Knupp","Murali Kondragunta","Garwin Lechner","Dominik Lê-Kellner","Jurek Leonhardt","Jiaze Li","Sohir Maskey","Omar Mehio","Pit Neitemeier","Letitia Parcalabescu","Alejandro Petry Pacheco","Pranav Ragupathy","Martin Reinhardt","Peter Rodenkirch Nfissi","Dylan Rodriquez","Leonard Salewski","Raziye Sari","Jordan Sassoon","Ivo Schaper","Ilhan Scheer","Frank Schneider","Philipp Scholl","Lukas Schott","Eric Schreiber","Pablo Schumacher","Alessio Serra","Philipp Siedler","Prabhu Teja Sivaprasad","Mohamed Soliman","Till Speicher","Volker Stampa","Simon Thel","Serge Thilges","Irina Vidal Migallón","Denis Waßmann","Samuel Weinbach","Sascha Wirges","Michael Wüthrich","Alexei Zacharov"],"tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Tool Use","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.31945.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.31945","abstract":"This report introduces Kolibri, an English-German mixture-of-experts transformer with 78.1B total parameters and 3.46B active parameters per token, released as open weights under the Apache 2.0 licence. We built Kolibri for sovereign and specialised deployment, with a particular focus on German and on regulated domains such as public administration, industry, and aerospace. To support these settings, we control the full model-development process, including data, architecture, training infrastructure, post-training, and evaluation. We train Kolibri on 24T tokens across pre-training, mid-training, and long-context extension, with German accounting for more than 20% of the data mix by bytes. To reach this share, we complement German datasets with more than 2T German tokens that we curate from the web with our own pipeline and generate synthetically. Post-training combines supervised fine-tu"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"TGR-EvoDAG: A DAG-based Multi-Agent Code Evolution Framework for Generative Recommendation on Tencent","topics":["LLM Training","Multi-Agent","Planning","Evaluation","Alignment","Reflection"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32326","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32326","authors":["Junlin He","Junzhang Zhu","Yudong Li","Yihong Tang","Xuefeng Jiang","Wei Ma","Chengxiang Zhuo","Zang Li"],"tags":["LLM Training","Multi-Agent","Planning","Evaluation","Alignment"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32326.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32326","abstract":"Large Language Model (LLM) agents have enabled automated code evolution for algorithm discovery. However, current recursive self-improvement (RSI) research for sequential recommendation faces a dilemma: general-purpose frameworks lack domain-specific feedback, while recommender-specific frameworks rely on linear evolution. Crucially, naively integrating them fails when applied to Generative Recommendation (GR). Unlike end-to-end models, GR relies on a module-cascaded pipeline (tokenization, pre-training, and alignment). In this paradigm, existing evolution methods suffer from: i) cost blindness, squandering budgets by ignoring stage-asymmetric retraining overheads; and ii) short-sighted evaluation, discarding promising upstream innovations that temporarily regress metrics before downstream adaptation. To bridge these gaps, we propose TGR-EvoDAG, a multi-agent code evolution framework tai"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"ImageRSI: Verification-Driven Recursive Self-Improvement for Agentic Image Generation","topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Evaluation","Fine-tuning","Reflection"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32120","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32120","authors":["Shuo Han","Zhilin Wang","Peng Ling","Wenjia Gong","Yang Liu","Guanjun Jiang","Yukun Cao","Xike Xie"],"tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Evaluation","Fine-tuning","Reflection"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32120.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32120","abstract":"Complex text-to-image requests remain difficult to satisfy reliably. Verifying and repairing an image can fix an individual output, but a successful repair does not necessarily carry over to other requests. We present ImageRSI, a framework that keeps the image generator frozen and combines three components: five-dimensional verification and repair within each task, an experience library shared across tasks, and offline updating of the judge. Candidate experiences enter the library only after paired A/B validation, and the judge is updated with supervised fine-tuning and GRPO on accumulated image-judgment records and evaluated before reuse. We also introduce ImageReq-Bench, a benchmark of 961 requests built around hard cases in real-world image-generation requests, each paired with an evaluation checklist. ImageRSI achieves a Checklist Strict Accuracy (CSA) of 52.6% on MindBench (with Nan"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"MusicTwins: Training Audio-Language Models to Listen like Their Persona","topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Evaluation","Fine-tuning","RL for LLM"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32495","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32495","authors":["Milan Liessens Dujardin","Kevin Miao"],"tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Evaluation","Fine-tuning","RL for LLM"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32495.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32495","abstract":"People differ in how they perceive music, what associations they make with it, what they like about it, and how they respond to it. Computational models intended to simulate people in environments that include music should account for these differences. Although LLMs have been shown to reproduce aspects of human behavior, whether audio-language models (ALMs) can reproduce a specific person's responses to textual and musical stimuli remains underexplored. We introduce MusicTwins, a framework that combines persona summaries, personal memory, and post-training to generate person-specific responses. We construct persona representations from human writings, capturing background, views, personality, writing style, and a musical profile, and post-train Audio Flamingo Next through supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning. Evaluation on ten held-out personas shows notable improvements fo"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"SOPO: Socratic Guided Policy Optimization for Span-Level Hallucination Detection","topics":["Post-Training","Reasoning","Hallucination","Evaluation","RL for LLM"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32212","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32212","authors":["Jiawei Dong","Zilong Bai","Pengtian zhu","Peng Zhou"],"tags":["Post-Training","Reasoning","Hallucination","Evaluation","RL for LLM"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32212.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32212","abstract":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have gained widespread adoption in various natural language processing tasks, but suffer from hallucination issues where they generate unfaithful or inconsistent content. While Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) has shown promising applications in reasoning tasks, its application to span-level hallucination detection is challenged by localization ambiguity, reward sparsity on hard examples, and uncontrolled length inflation. To address these issues, we introduce Socratic Guided Policy Optimization (SOPO), a multi-turn RL framework for robust hallucination detection. The key innovation of SOPO is a Socratic feedback mechanism, where a teacher model guides the reasoning trajectories during rollout by providing indirect hints inferred from the ground truth. This teacher-guided approach enables the model to generate more effective reasoning tra"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"Semantically Rich, Metrically Affect-Blind: A Geometric Blind Spot in Modern Text Embedders","topics":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","LLM Training","Alignment","Data Engine"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32122","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32122","authors":["Mahdi Naser Moghadasi"],"tags":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","LLM Training","Alignment","Data Engine"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32122.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32122","abstract":"We report a systematic dissociation in modern text embedders: sentiment is almost perfectly decodable from their embeddings, yet almost entirely absent from their metric. Across eleven frozen encoders spanning 2021 to 2026, including 2026-generation models and the 4B-parameter LLM embedder Qwen3-Embedding-4B, a linear probe recovers sentence polarity at up to 91.3% accuracy while cosine similarity separates polarity by a margin of only 0.003-0.064. Qwen3-Embedding-4B, the strongest probe in the panel, reaches 91.3% probe accuracy but only 64.3 retrieval MAP. Because retrieval, clustering, deduplication, and retrieval-augmented generation operate on the metric rather than on a trained probe, these systems are effectively blind to information their encoder demonstrably contains. A task-conditioning control suggests this reflects the pretraining objective rather than a prompting artifact: c"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"StateBridge: Phase- and Track-Grounded Reasoning for Synthetic-to-Real Traffic Video Captioning and VQA","topics":["LLM Training","VLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31898","submittedDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31898","authors":["Blessing Agyei Kyem","Richard Dzinyela","Joshua Kofi Asamoah","Eugene Denteh","Andrews Danyo","Solomon Owusu-Ansah","Dickson Gyimah Asiedu","Armstrong Aboah"],"tags":["LLM Training","VLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.31898.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.31898","abstract":"Traffic safety videos provide rich evidence for understanding interactions between pedestrians and vehicles, but synthetic-to-real evaluation makes this setting challenging because models must learn from simulated scenes and generalize to real-world traffic. In this work, we present StateBridge, a traffic video-language framework designed to operate under this synthetic-only training protocol. Our approach uses phase-aware clip construction, pedestrian and vehicle track summaries, and structured prompts to ground vision-language models in stable traffic evidence rather than appearance alone. We fine-tune public vision-language models on synthetic captioning and visual question answering examples, then combine their outputs through fact-locked caption fusion and evidence-diverse VQA consensus to improve consistency across actor roles, behavioral phases, and question types. StateBridge ach"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"Tempo-Zero: Recursive Self-Play in Executable Worlds for Video Reasoning","topics":["LLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning","Reflection"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32433","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32433","authors":["Jingmin Zhu","Yanyan Zou","Xiuyu Li","James Bailey","Dinh Phung","Tao Yu","Yunyun Hou","Jun Liu","Jun Zhou","Sirui Wang","Nan Duan","Jiachen Liu","Qiuhong Ke"],"tags":["LLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning","Reflection"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32433.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32433","abstract":"Video reasoning requires understanding how events unfold over time, yet videos with accurate temporal annotations are expensive to collect. Self-play offers an alternative by letting a model generate and solve its own tasks without manual annotation. However, existing video self-play methods train on fixed sets of recorded videos, so they cannot alter the underlying event dynamics and rely on noisy pseudo-labels derived from the model's own outputs, such as majority voting, that risk reinforcing shared model errors. To overcome these limitations, we introduce Tempo-Zero, a self-improvement framework that closes a loop of executable environments, execution-verified self-play, and environment evolution, where executing a program renders a video with the ground-truth option and interval of every question. A single policy plays both Questioner and Solver, proposing questions and answering th"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"PCFBench: How Far Can Large Vision-Language Models Go in Physics-aware Photonic Inverse Design?","topics":["VLM Training","Multi-Agent","Reasoning","Code Agent","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32485","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32485","authors":["Shengchao Chen","Ting Shu","Sufen Ren"],"tags":["VLM Training","Multi-Agent","Reasoning","Code Agent","Evaluation"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32485.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32485","abstract":"Photonic crystal fibers (PCFs) underpin critical applications in telecommunications, sensing, and high-power laser delivery, yet their design remains a manual, expert-intensive process that couples geometric intuition with deep waveguide physics. Vision-language models (VLMs) offer a promising path toward automating PCF design, but can they truly reason about photonic physics from microscopy images, or do they merely recognize visual patterns? We introduce PCFBench, the first comprehensive benchmark for evaluating VLMs on the perception, reasoning, and decision steps of the photonic inverse-design loop. PCFBench spans 26 tasks organized into six categories of increasing cognitive demand (Geometry Perception, Physics Understanding, Text Generation, Multi-modal Reasoning, Inverse-Design Decision, and Code Generation), built from over 220K FDTD simulation samples across 18 structurally dive"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"Astro2Method: Benchmarking LLMs on Machine Learning Method Selection in Astronomy","topics":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30282","submittedDate":"2026-10-21","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30282","authors":["Xingzhong Fan","Hangfeng He","Fatemeh Nargesian"],"tags":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.30282.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.30282","abstract":"Machine learning (ML) is increasingly used in astronomy, but choosing a suitable method for a scientific problem often requires expertise in both astronomy and ML. Large language models (LLMs) could help astronomers make such choices, yet this ability remains underexplored in existing benchmarks. We introduce Astro2Method, a benchmark of 500 expert-reviewed binary questions from 346 astronomy papers (2024/01-2026/06), based on clear performance differences and complete (Easy) or partial (Hard) explanations from the authors. Across five LLMs, direct prompting achieves 87.50-92.55% accuracy on Easy questions but only 58.87-64.52% on Hard. Using historical papers, neither retrieval-augmented generation nor most agent methods with additional reasoning or retrieval (including ReAct, Self-Ask, and CRITIC) bring consistent improvement. Agents required to search the web improve accuracy mainly w"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"RxnExBench: Benchmarking Vision-Language Models for Structured Chemical Reaction Extraction from Scientific Literature","topics":["VLM Training","Reasoning","Planning","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32221","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32221","authors":["Jiaxing Sun","Junyuan Gao","Ruikang Zhang","Hao Zheng","Lingli Ge","Xiao An","Jiang Wu","Huazheng Zeng","Jiahe Song","Chen Zhu","Haote Yang","Jingchao Wang","Lu Chen","Yuanyuan Cao","Ruijie Zhang","Shengxin Lu","Weijia Li","Lijun Wu","Conghui He"],"tags":["VLM Training","Reasoning","Planning","Evaluation"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32221.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32221","abstract":"Screen Tables and Substrate Scopes are information-dense figures widely used in synthetic chemistry literature, yet automated extraction of structured reaction data from them remains challenging. We introduce RxnExBench, a multimodal benchmark for fine-grained reaction-level evaluation. Built from high-impact chemistry journals, it contains 3,565 expert-annotated reactions and 2,361 molecular structures. The dataset adopts a unified schema with 15 reaction roles and 26 core fields, supporting downstream AI-for-Chemistry tasks such as retrosynthetic planning, yield prediction, and reaction condition recommendation. RxnExBench further provides a reasoning-driven difficulty taxonomy, a modular annotation pipeline, and the Oracle-MolRec protocol, which disentangles molecular recognition from higher-level reasoning. Evaluation of 9 state-of-the-art VLMs shows that performance degrades substan"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"$Ψ$-0.6: Adaptive Streams of Thought in a Unified Vision-Language World Model","topics":["Reasoning","Evaluation","RL for LLM","World Model"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32177","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32177","authors":["Khai Loong Aw","Klemen Kotar","Baihan Zhang","Yifan Liu","Lilian Chen","Atlas Kazemian","Rahul Venkatesh","Daniel Yamins"],"tags":["Reasoning","Evaluation","RL for LLM","World Model"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32177.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32177","abstract":"Humans reason about the physical world by flexibly combining different forms of inference as a problem unfolds. Achieving this flexibility is a major open challenge in AI: capabilities often remain separated across specialized models, limiting how they can be composed and jointly adapted to a task. We introduce $Ψ$-0.6, a unified vision-language world model that adaptively composes perception, language, geometry, and simulation within a shared reasoning process we call streams of thought. These streams interleave images, text, and explicit representations of geometry and motion, all of which the model can generate and interpret through a shared token interface. $Ψ$-0.6 surpasses leading open-weight models zero-shot on multi-view spatial reasoning, with further gains from reasoning through geometric intermediates. It also supports counterfactual simulation: generating and interpreting ima"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"When to Stop Penalizing Actions? Martingale GRPO for VLA Reinforcement Learning","topics":["Post-Training","VLM Training","Fine-tuning","RL for LLM"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32431","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32431","authors":["Zongyao Zhao","Cheng-Jie Zhao","Ruihang Jiang","Bing Zheng","Zhaolin Wang","Yuanwei Liu"],"tags":["Post-Training","VLM Training","Fine-tuning","RL for LLM"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32431.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32431","abstract":"A failed vision-language-action (VLA) rollout does not imply that every action contributed to failure. Yet in group-relative policy optimization (GRPO), a single trajectory-level advantage is assigned to all actions in a rollout. Potentially useful recovery attempts can thus be penalized alongside the errors they aim to correct. Ideal stopping conditions for failure feedback are characterized using success-probability and policy-score martingales. At these stops, the removed gradient contribution has zero conditional expectation given the retained information. Truncation therefore preserves the expectation of a reference GRPO group-gradient estimator and can reduce its covariance. One such stop occurs when the conditional probability of success is zero, motivating error and recovery cues for selecting candidate feedback boundaries. Martingale GRPO (MART-GRPO) is introduced with a vision-"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"Solvent: Long-Horizon Reasoning for Multi-Step Retrosynthesis","topics":["Post-Training","Reasoning","Planning","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32308","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32308","authors":["Yifei Wang","Botian Wang","YiFei Wang","Jiangtao Feng","Xinbo Zhang","Yawen Ouyang","Yixin Wu","Lihao Wang","Xiaoqing Zheng","Xuanjing Huang","Hao Zhou"],"tags":["Post-Training","Reasoning","Planning","Evaluation"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32308.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32308","abstract":"Multi-step retrosynthesis requires recursively transforming a target molecule through successive reaction steps into purchasable building blocks, and recent work has begun to draw on large language model (LLM) reasoning to tackle the task. In its latest evolution, LLM reasoning has entered the long-horizon regime: models pursue objectives across interdependent steps, interact with environments, and adapt to feedback. This shift has markedly advanced LLM capabilities, as illustrated by breakthroughs in AI-driven mathematical research. At this frontier, we introduce Solvent, an 8B LLM that successfully extends long-horizon reasoning to multi-step retrosynthesis. Through a two-round post-training curriculum, Solvent learns to interleave iterative synthesis planning, external availability verification, and adaptive route revision. Experiments show that, following a $2.1\\times$ performance bo"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"From Visual Affinity to Learning Credit in Multimodal Reinforcement Learning","topics":["Post-Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","RL for LLM"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32324","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32324","authors":["Yuhang Han","Dingning Liu","Yujie Chen","Yaojie Zhang","Jingwei Song","Zequn Yang","Xuanming Shang","Li Ding","Zhijiang Guo","Linfeng Zhang"],"tags":["Post-Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","RL for LLM"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32324.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32324","abstract":"Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) improves multimodal reasoning, but uniformly assigning a single response-level advantage to all tokens overlooks the distinct roles of visual observations and subsequent reasoning. Visual affinity alone is insufficient: predictive states may align with both image and prompt-context representations, and upweighting valid visual observations can amplify their negative-advantage updates when later reasoning fails. To address this issue, we propose \\textbf{Outcome-Conditioned Visual Credit (OVC)}, a plug-in framework that separates visual-association measurement from token-level credit assignment and is adaptable to policy-optimization methods with uniform response-level advantage allocation. Relative Visual Encoding constructs a context-relative visual coordinate by contrasting each predictive state's affinity to image and prompt-context"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"Efficient Zero-Shot Video Anomaly Detection via Signal-Aware Adaptive Fusion (SAAF)","topics":["LLM Training","VLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32486","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32486","authors":["Khaled Waleed Dawoud","Muhammad Haris Khan"],"tags":["LLM Training","VLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32486.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32486","abstract":"Deploying Vision-Language Models for video anomaly detection confronts a fundamental constraint. Their semantic reasoning demands computational budgets incompatible with the scale of real-world video streams. We present Signal-Aware Adaptive Fusion (SAAF), a framework that achieves efficient zero-shot anomaly detection through three key innovations. First, we introduce a dual-signal adaptive sampler operating in semantic embedding space: a delta signal captures sudden events via frame-to-frame cosine distance, while a drift signal detects static and gradual anomalies through deviation from rolling temporal context. Their OR-gated combination with adaptive EMA-based thresholding provides comprehensive anomaly coverage without training data. Second, we propose a threshold-gated cascaded architecture where lightweight frame-level scoring precedes expensive video-level temporal analysis, inv"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"An Agentic Approach with Evidence-Grounded Verification for Multimodal Emotion Reasoning in Conversation","topics":["Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning","Reflection"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31685","submittedDate":"2026-10-03","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31685","authors":["Elyas Rashno","Farhana Zulkernine"],"tags":["Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning","Reflection"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.31685.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.31685","abstract":"LLM-based multimodal emotion reasoning systems perform well on standard benchmarks, but improving them through instruction tuning can be computationally expensive. Agentic pipelines built from frozen models avoid this cost, but introduce a different challenge: when a prediction is wrong, the system must identify which agent or stage caused the error. We address this problem with an agentic framework for traceable multimodal emotion reasoning and prompt-space adaptation. Modality-specific perception agents analyze visual, acoustic, and lexical inputs and post evidence-cited observations to a shared board. An integration agent combines these observations with retrieved multimodal examples to produce an emotion description, label set, and supporting citations, while an MLLM critic can request one revision before the output is finalized. For incorrect predictions, a separate evidence-grounde"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"Learning to Be Monitored: Causal Bounds and Intervention-Aware Post-Training Recipes for Chain-of-Thought Monitorability","topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31733","submittedDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31733","authors":["Qiang Fu","Xinyue Li","Andre Wibisono","Arman Cohan"],"tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.31733.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.31733","abstract":"Chain-of-thought (CoT) monitoring aims to detect potentially harmful behaviors in large language models (LLMs) from their CoTs. Intervention-based evaluations measure this property by asking whether a monitor can detect the role of an intervention from the CoT when the targeted behavior occurs, typically using minimal-criterion sensitivity ($\\mathsf{TPR_{mc}}$) proposed by~\\citep{guan2025monitoring}. However, a target behavior observed under intervention is not necessarily caused by it, and we show that this metric can overestimate how often a monitor detects target behaviors actually caused by the intervention. Specifically, we establish $\\mathsf{TPR_{mc}}$ as an upper bound on this causal detection rate and derive a complementary lower bound, which provides a tighter evaluation framework for CoT monitorability. With this framework, we study whether LLMs can be trained to make their CoT"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"Decoupling Patient Personas and Clinical Workflows: A Dual-Agent Approach to Realistic Medical Dialogue Generation","topics":["Alignment","Fine-tuning","Data Engine","Agent Framework"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32443","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32443","authors":["Qiyuan Li","Xiaomei Guo","Chao Gao","Haijiang Liu","Deyu Chen","Caicai Guo","Jinguang Gu"],"tags":["Alignment","Fine-tuning","Data Engine","Agent Framework"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32443.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32443","abstract":"Pre-consultation medical dialogues are essential for collecting comprehensive patient information, but are costly to obtain at scale due to privacy and ethical constraints. Existing LLM-based synthesis methods often rely on prompt engineering or rigid rules, limiting their ability to balance clinical relevance with realistic patient diversity. We propose CoRPS-Dial, a controllable dual-agent framework for pre-consultation medical dialogue synthesis that integrates patient personas with guideline-aligned clinical workflow rules. CoRPS-Dial generates dialogues through interactions between a persona-driven patient agent and a process-guided doctor agent, followed by post-hoc quality control for role consistency, workflow completeness, and textual consistency. Experiments on coronary heart disease demonstrate that CoRPS-Dial improves alignment between synthetic and real dialogues in informat"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"ReMedI: Inference-Time Refinement and Self-Distillation for Controllable Molecular Optimization","topics":["Post-Training","Evaluation","Alignment","Fine-tuning"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32103","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32103","authors":["Jia Yi Zhang","Shivanshu Shekhar","Allison McLevige","Tong Zhang"],"tags":["Post-Training","Evaluation","Alignment","Fine-tuning"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32103.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32103","abstract":"In drug development, lead optimization must improve competing properties, such as increasing permeability and reducing toxicity, while limiting scaffold changes that could compromise the lead's potency. RL-based post-training, such as C-MORAL, addresses this problem by fine-tuning molecular language models to directly optimize molecular leads for objectives of interest, which relies on expensive task-specific datasets and faces challenges in achieving multi-objective optimization (MOO). We argue a pre-trained model may already contain useful molecular edits that zero-shot generation cannot efficiently reach. Motivated by the limited search capabilities of zero-shot generation, we develop an efficient Metropolis-Hastings search strategy to better explore the proposal distribution. We propose ReMedI, which uses the molecular language model as a base proposer for inference-time alignment. G"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"4DMemory: Benchmarking Long-Horizon Spatiotemporal Memory with Harness-Augmented Agents","topics":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32332","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32332","authors":["Yanhao Jia","Yi Zhu","Yiran Zhong","Weigao Sun","Feida Zhu","Pengkun Jiao","Sihan Yang","Ming Ma","Steven Hoi","Erik Cambria"],"tags":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","Reasoning","Evaluation"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32332.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32332","abstract":"Humans efficiently process multimodal streams spanning hours to years, building persistent, fine-grained spatiotemporal memories that support reasoning about and predicting long-horizon events. In contrast, existing benchmarks focus on short- and medium-context tasks, and most foundation models struggle to preserve fine-grained information over long horizons. To evaluate and improve long-horizon spatiotemporal reasoning, we introduce the LongVideo dataset and the 4DMemoryBench. LongVideo comprises about 900 hours of video and over 5,000 manually reviewed questions covering visual, textual, and spatiotemporal reasoning across diverse context lengths. We further develop the 4DMemory Harness, which integrates four complementary skills, 22+ specialized tool models, and a model-specific retrieval-augmented generation system. Evaluating 13 leading models, we find that standalone foundation mod"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"Omni-UBench: Benchmarking Omni-Modal LLM-based Guardrails Across Safety, Utility, and Reliability","topics":["Agent Safety","Hallucination","Evaluation","Alignment"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31713","submittedDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31713","authors":["Junhao Wang","Yidou Chen","Li Lu","Hongwei Yao","Xiaodan Li","Mengjie Wu","Yuefeng Chen","Zhan Qin","Kui Ren"],"tags":["Agent Safety","Hallucination","Evaluation","Alignment"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.31713.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.31713","abstract":"Advances in Omni-modal Large Language Models (OLLMs) have enabled increasingly seamless human-AI interaction, but their unified processing of heterogeneous modalities also expands the attack surface, allowing adversaries to exploit richer input compositions to circumvent safety alignment. To counter such risks, omni-modal guardrails have been proposed to detect and filter unsafe queries across arbitrary modalities. However, their capabilities and limitations remain insufficiently understood due to the absence of a comprehensive evaluation benchmark. In this paper, we present Omni-UBench, the first comprehensive framework for omni-modal safety assessment, evaluating guardrail models along three critical dimensions, i.e., safety, utility, and reliability. Omni-UBench curates over 183k samples drawn from 35+ heterogeneous sources spanning text, image, audio, video, and their cross-modal com"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"Accordion: Elastic-Depth Inference via Fixed-Prefix Layer Looping","topics":["LLM Training","Reasoning","Fine-tuning","RL for LLM"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32492","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32492","authors":["Yudong Zhang","Wei Ding","Kaili Zheng","Yuchen Ding","Zhenyu Zhang"],"tags":["LLM Training","Reasoning","Fine-tuning","RL for LLM"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32492.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32492","abstract":"Large language models serve applications with diverse inference budgets, but training and maintaining separate models for each budget incurs substantial costs. Recent Looped Transformers enable flexible inference computation through parameter reuse, but maintaining strong performance across different inference budgets remains an open challenge. We introduce Accordion, a mixture-of-experts-based model that supports five inference budgets through fixed-prefix layer looping. Each budget follows a pre-defined execution path, while the minimum budget retains the unlooped model. Joint pretraining samples one budget per micro-batch and optimizes its next-token prediction loss without additional cross-budget consistency objectives. We further preserve these inference budgets through supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning, where parameter-group merging and budget-matched multi-teacher "},{"upvotes":0,"title":"Aligning Large Language Models with Human Feedback: Mathematical Foundations and Algorithm Design","topics":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Alignment","Fine-tuning"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31862","submittedDate":"2026-10-22","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31862","authors":["Siliang Zeng","Luca Viano","Chenliang Li","Jiaxiang Li","Volkan Cevher","Markus Wulfmeier","Stefano Ermon","Alfredo Garcia","Mingyi Hong"],"tags":["Post-Training","LLM Training","Alignment","Fine-tuning"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.31862.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.31862","abstract":"This article provides an introduction to the mathematical foundations and algorithmic frameworks used to align Large Language Models (LLMs) with human intentions, preferences, and values. We discuss standard alignment techniques, such as fine-tuning (SFT), reinforcement learning with human feedback (RLHF), and direct preference optimization (DPO). We also explore the theoretical underpinnings of learning from human preferences, drawing connections to inverse reinforcement learning (IRL) and discrete choice models. We present state-of-the-art algorithms in a tutorial style, discuss their advantages and limitations, and offer insights into practical implementation. Our exposition is intended to serve as a comprehensive resource for researchers and practitioners, providing both a foundational understanding of alignment methodologies and a framework for developing more robust and scalable al"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"Profile-Driven User Simulation via Reinforcement Learning with Hierarchical Rewards for Task-Oriented Dialogues","topics":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","Post-Training","Fine-tuning"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32435","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32435","authors":["Juan Li","Fangshu Chang","Xinyi Jiang","Ling Huang","Yunzhang Chen"],"tags":["Agentic RAG","Agentic Search","Post-Training","Fine-tuning"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32435.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32435","abstract":"Building Task-Oriented Dialogue (TOD) systems for high-value scenarios such as financial marketing requires large volumes of domain-specific dialogue data, which privacy regulations, annotation costs, and the need for expert labor make hard to obtain. User simulation offers a remedy, but existing methods target general-domain dialogue and exhibit two key limitations: insufficient modeling of domain-specific business processes and compliance constraints, and inadequate capture of the colloquial, spoken-language character of voice interactions. We propose ProfileSim, a profile-driven user simulation framework for financial marketing outbound calls. It jointly models user profiles---demographics and Big Five personality traits---and business profiles---intents reverse-engineered from real call logs---enabling fine-grained control over simulated behavior. The base model is fine-tuned on real"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"SyncPro: Synchronous Reasoning and Responding for Proactive Streaming Video Understanding","topics":["LLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32132","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32132","authors":["Xueyun Tian","Bingbing Xu","Jiaming Liu","Jinpeng Yu","Yang Shi","Yu Zhao","Ruihua Huang","Yuanzhuo Wang","Huawei Shen"],"tags":["LLM Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","Fine-tuning"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32132.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32132","abstract":"Proactive streaming video understanding requires assistants to track unfolding events and respond when a user's requested condition is met. To make these decisions, assistants must accumulate relevant evidence and reassess response conditions as new observations arrive. Existing work offers complementary capabilities for this process: proactive video assistants learn when to respond, while streaming video reasoning methods generate scheduled intermediate text to organize observations and support subsequent answers. Together, these capabilities provide a foundation for proactive reasoning, but how to coordinate ongoing reasoning with timely responses remains underexplored. In a direct adaptation, fixed reasoning intervals can trigger updates when nothing relevant has changed or postpone useful updates until the next scheduled step. Sharing one generation path also couples two distinct req"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"MS-TTRL: Breaking the Frequency Conflict in Multiple-Solution Test-Time Reinforcement Learning","topics":["Post-Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","RL for LLM"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32234","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32234","authors":["Zhixia Zhang","Binqian Xu","Huaiyang Wang","Yikun Ban","Dianbo Liu"],"tags":["Post-Training","Reasoning","Evaluation","RL for LLM"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32234.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32234","abstract":"Scientific, engineering, and mathematical reasoning tasks can admit multiple valid solutions, while exhaustive annotation of the full solution set is often prohibitively costly. Test-time reinforcement learning (TTRL) applies reinforcement learning to unlabeled test instances, offering a label-free approach to multiple-solution reasoning. In this setting, generation frequency serves two opposing objectives: correctness estimation favors frequent outcomes, whereas outcome-level diversity preservation favors less frequent outcomes. We term this tension the \\textbf{Frequency Conflict}. To resolve this conflict, we propose MS-TTRL, which obtains frequency-independent correctness estimates through Outcome-Level Self-Verification while reserving generation frequency solely for diversity preservation. Three-State Outcome Cache and the Candidate State support verification reuse across repeated o"},{"upvotes":0,"title":"DT-JEPA: Learning to Predict Future Representations for Decision-Making","topics":["LLM Training","Evaluation","Fine-tuning","World Model"],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32657","submittedDate":"2026-10-23","source":"arxiv","announceDate":"2026-10-26","arxiv":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32657","authors":["Changxu Ni","Yiyang Ni","Jun Li","Feiran You","Hongyang Du","Haitao Zhao","Shi Jin"],"tags":["LLM Training","Evaluation","Fine-tuning","World Model"],"date":"2026-10-26","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.32657.pdf","id":"arxiv:2610.32657","abstract":"Decision Transformer (DT) has demonstrated strong performance in various offline reinforcement learning (RL) tasks by conditioning action generation on expected future returns and historical trajectories. However, treating expected future returns as the condition provides an incomplete description of the future, as trajectories with different state transitions and behaviors may share similar returns. Moreover, expected future returns inherently depend on task-specific reward functions, which makes policy learning reliant on reward-labeled datasets and restricts its scalability to tasks with similar but unseen rewards. Recently, Joint Embedding Predictive Architectures (JEPA) have emerged as a promising paradigm of self-supervised learning-based world models, which learn predictive representations of future dynamics in latent space rather than reconstructing future states in the original "}],"archive":[],"latestPath":"data/latest.json"};
